# LibroDigital.pe — environment template.
#
# Copy to a file named by `ENV_FILE` and keep that file OUTSIDE this repository:
#
#   ENV_FILE=%USERPROFILE%\.librodigital\dev.env     (Windows dev machine)
#   ENV_FILE=/etc/librodigital/app.env               (VPS, mode 640 root)
#
# Why not just `.env` in the project? Anything inside the workspace is readable
# by every editor, backup, tarball and coding agent that is allowed to open the
# project. `ENV_FILE` is how the passwords are kept out of that reach.
# Unset, the server falls back to `<repo>/.env` (development only).
#
# In production nothing on disk is needed at all: inject the variables from the
# platform's secret store and they take precedence over any file. See
# RUNBOOK.md section 0.1 ("Where the secrets physically live").
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` are git-ignored; this template is not.
# ENV_FILE=

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string (Neon serverless in production).
# Locally it may be omitted entirely: the app then falls back to an embedded
# PGlite database in `.local-db`. Use `pglite:<dir>` to choose another location.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# Signs the session cookie (`lv.sid`). REQUIRED in production: the server
# refuses to boot without it (see server/middleware/security.ts).
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# Absolute base URL of the deployment. Every outgoing email link (welcome,
# password reset, SLA reminders) is built from it, so it must be the real
# domain — otherwise customers receive links to localhost.
APP_URL=https://librodigital.pe

# --- Reverse proxy / CDN (Cloudflare) ---------------------------------------
#
# librodigital.pe is served through Cloudflare, so the connection the Node
# process sees comes from Cloudflare, not from the consumer. `trust proxy` is
# what tells Express to read the real client address from `X-Forwarded-For`
# instead of using Cloudflare's IP for everyone — without it the login limiter
# would count the whole country as one visitor and the security log would be
# useless. See server/middleware/security.ts.
#
# Accepted values:
#   1                  (default) one hop in front of the app — Cloudflare
#                      talking to the origin directly.
#   2                  Cloudflare plus your host's own proxy (the platform edge
#                      adds a second `X-Forwarded-For` entry).
#   <n>                any explicit hop count.
#   173.245.48.0/20,…  a comma-separated CIDR list: the safest form, because
#                      only those peers may set the header. Paste Cloudflare's
#                      published ranges (https://www.cloudflare.com/ips/), e.g.
#                      the IPv4 set plus the IPv6 set.
#   false              trust nobody (only correct when the app is exposed
#                      directly, with no proxy at all).
#
# Keep the origin firewalled to Cloudflare's ranges; otherwise the header can be
# spoofed by anyone who finds the origin IP.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- Email (SMTP) -----------------------------------------------------------

# Optional locally: when absent, emails are printed to the terminal instead of
# being sent. Required in production, otherwise no welcome or password-reset
# email ever reaches a customer and /api/status reports `email: degraded`.
#
# Zoho Mail is the mail host for librodigital.pe (the DNS lives in Cloudflare -
# see the "Cloudflare + Zoho Mail" section of RUNBOOK.md for the SPF/DKIM/DMARC
# records that make the mail deliverable).
#
# SMTP_PASSWORD depends on the account:
#   * 2FA/TFA NOT enabled -> the normal mailbox password.
#   * 2FA/TFA enabled     -> an app-specific password, generated in ZOHO ACCOUNTS
#     (accounts.zoho.com -> Seguridad -> Contraseñas de la aplicación ->
#      Generar nueva contraseña), NOT inside Zoho Mail. Shown once only.
#
# SMTP_HOST/PORT depend on the plan and the data centre:
#   * any plan       -> smtp.zoho.com     port 587 (STARTTLS)
#   * paid org       -> smtppro.zoho.com  port 465 (SSL)
#   * free/personal  -> smtp.zoho.com     port 465 (SSL)
#   * .eu / .in DCs  -> smtp.zoho.eu / smtp.zoho.in (+ their smtppro.* forms)
# Authoritative per-account values: Zoho Mail -> Configuración -> Cuentas de
# correo -> your address -> "Detalles de configuración del servidor".
#
# Verify instead of guessing: `npm run email:diagnose` tries every documented
# combination and reports the first that authenticates (add
# `-- --send tu@correo.com` to deliver a real message). Any other SMTP provider
# works too - only SMTP_HOST and SMTP_PORT change.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.zoho.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=hola@librodigital.pe
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_FROM_NAME=LibroDigital.pe
SMTP_FROM_EMAIL=hola@librodigital.pe

# Only needed when the port does not imply it (465 implies SSL). Set to "false"
# for Zoho on port 587, which uses STARTTLS instead.
# SMTP_SECURE=false

# When SMTP credentials are absent, `npm run dev` does not send mail: it prints
# the message (generated password, reset link and all) to the terminal so the auth
# flows stay usable offline. That capture mode is an OPT-IN (development / test, or
# EMAIL_CAPTURE=true) and never inferred from `NODE_ENV !== "production"`, because
# an unset NODE_ENV in production would then print credentials into the logs while
# silently delivering nothing. Set EMAIL_CAPTURE=true to force it anywhere else.
# EMAIL_CAPTURE=true

# Leave the default (on) for Zoho or any host with a trusted certificate. Set
# to "false" only for a self-signed mail server, e.g. some shared hosts.
# SMTP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=false

# --- Commercial lifecycle ---------------------------------------------------

# Email verification is ENFORCED (server/services/verificationService.ts): a
# business publishes its Libro de Reclamaciones only once an active administrator
# of that tenant has confirmed their mailbox, and no company can be created from
# an unconfirmed account (403 email_not_verified). The mailbox matters because it
# is where every complaint notification - and the 15-business-day clock - lands.
#
# Set to "true" ONLY to keep a legacy tenant whose book is already live working
# while it confirms: it disables the check both at onboarding and at the public
# intake, so a mistyped address can start missing complaint notices silently.
# ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_BUSINESSES=false

# --- AI (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------

# Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Without a key the AI features fall back to
# local heuristics: they degrade, they do not break.
AI_API_KEY=
AI_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
AI_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
AI_MODEL_FAST=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# Model used by the compliance co-pilot tool-calling loop; defaults to AI_MODEL.
# AI_AGENT_MODEL=

# --- Payments (Peruvian PSPs) -----------------------------------------------

# Stripe does not onboard Peruvian companies and PayPal is barely used locally,
# so the two relevant rails are a Peruvian PSP or a plain bank transfer.
#
# Mercado Pago (Checkout Pro + Suscripciones) is the recommended provider: one
# integration covers tarjetas, Yape, Plin and cash, and "Suscripciones" makes the
# charge repeat every period so a monthly/yearly plan renews by itself.
# Get the access token in "Sus integraciones > Datos de integración". Use the TEST
# credentials first and set MERCADOPAGO_SANDBOX=true so the checkout uses the
# sandbox URL.
MERCADOPAGO_ACCESS_TOKEN=
MERCADOPAGO_SANDBOX=false

# In the Mercado Pago panel, register
#   {APP_URL}/api/payments/webhook/mercadopago
# and subscribe to these events, otherwise renewals never reach the app:
#   - Pagos (payment)
#   - Suscripciones (subscription_preapproval)
#   - Pagos autorizados (subscription_authorized_payment)

# --- About the plans you create in the Mercado Pago panel ---------------------
#
# The panel plans are your public price list, and their codes are how a payment
# made through them is recognised (see PLAN_REFERENCE_CODES in shared/payments.ts).
#
# Do NOT hand out the panel's own subscription links (mpago.la/...). They carry no
# information about the customer, so activation becomes a guess about who paid.
# Share the link from "Plan y Cuotas" -> "Copiar enlace para compartir" instead:
#   {APP_URL}/suscribir?plan=<plan>&periodo=<monthly|annual>
# It identifies the customer first and brings them straight back to the payment.

# Culqi (tarjetas/Yape) is catalogued but not wired yet: it needs the Culqi
# Checkout SDK in the browser plus a CSP allow-list entry. Reserved for later.
CULQI_SECRET_KEY=

# Text shown to the customer on the "transferencia / Yape directo" checkout.
# Put your real account numbers here — it is customer-facing content.
#
# PREFER THE PANEL: the console's "Configuración" tab writes the same message from
# the database (platform_payment_settings) and takes precedence over this variable,
# so a Yape number or a new bank account no longer needs a deploy. This variable is
# the fallback used while that row is still empty - a fresh environment, or before
# an operator has opened the screen.
#
# Leave BOTH empty and the customer is only told to ask you for the details, which
# is a bad first impression for a paying customer. Suggested content:
#   PAYMENTS_MANUAL_INSTRUCTIONS=Yape al 999 888 777 (a nombre de LibroDigital S.A.C.)
#   Transferencia BCP - Cuenta Soles: 193-1234567-0-12
#   CCI: 002-193-001234567012-15
#   Titular: LibroDigital S.A.C. - RUC 20xxxxxxxxx
#   Envíe el comprobante a pagos@librodigital.pe
# (each line separated by the two characters \n inside the value)
PAYMENTS_MANUAL_INSTRUCTIONS=


# Who may confirm a bank transfer / Yape by hand (the "Confirmar pago recibido"
# button and POST /api/payments/:id/confirm). Comma-separated e-mails.
# NOT a tenant role: every customer is `admin` of their own business, so a role
# check would let a customer confirm their own unpaid transfer and get the plan
# for free. Empty = nobody can confirm, and paid transfers stay pending.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAILS=hola@librodigital.pe

# Where the team's console lives (`client/src/lib/admin-path.ts`). Deliberately NOT
# `/admin`: that is the first path every scanner and exploit bot tries. It lives
# under `/acceso/`, where signing in already happens. The client bakes it at build
# time and the server reads it for the `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` header, so both
# must hold the same value in a deployment. `/admin` itself stays a 404 on purpose.
VITE_ADMIN_PATH=/acceso/operaciones-7f31

# Escape hatch for manual invoicing: when "true", POST /api/subscription may
# change the plan WITHOUT collecting money (the pre-payments behaviour).
#
# PREFER THE PANEL: the console's "Configuración" tab has a switch for this, and every
# time it is turned on the change is written to `platform_config_audit` with the
# operator's e-mail. This variable stays as the deploy-level override; either one is
# enough to enable it. Leave both off in production, otherwise any logged-in admin can
# self-upgrade for free.
# PAYMENTS_ALLOW_FREE_PLAN_CHANGE=false

# Where to send operational payment alerts (a subscription that was paid but could
# not be matched to any account, because the payer used a different email than
# their app login). Without it the event is only logged.
#
# PREFER THE PANEL: Configuración -> Alertas stores the same address and takes
# precedence; this variable is the fallback while that field is empty. The same
# screen has a button that sends a real test message through SMTP.
# PAYMENTS_ALERT_EMAIL=hola@librodigital.pe

# --- WhatsApp Cloud API (optional add-on) -----------------------------------

# NOT needed for the standard flow: putting the Libro link in the business's
# WhatsApp/Instagram/TikTok profile (see "Su Libro en WhatsApp, Instagram,
# TikTok, YouTube y Facebook" in /integraciones) requires none of this.
#
# This section only powers an optional add-on: auto-answering inbound messages
# and notifying an already written response. It needs a Meta WhatsApp Business
# Platform account (WABA) with one number per business. Without these values the
# add-on degrades silently: no screen shows it and nothing is sent.
#
# Configure the webhook in "WhatsApp > Configuration" with
#   Callback URL: {APP_URL}/api/whatsapp/webhook
#   Verify token: the value of WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN below
# and subscribe to the "messages" field.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# App secret (Settings > Basic). REQUIRED in production: empty means the webhook
# cannot verify the X-Hub-Signature-256 HMAC and would accept forged payloads.
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v21.0
# Override only for testing (e.g. a local mock). Defaults to graph.facebook.com.
# WHATSAPP_GRAPH_BASE=https://graph.facebook.com

# --- RUC lookup (SUNAT) -----------------------------------------------------

# Powers "type the RUC, we fill the razón social" on the public registration
# form (`GET /api/lookup-ruc/:ruc`, see server/ruc-lookup.ts). Optional: with
# nothing configured here the lookup still runs anonymously, and if the service
# is down or saturated the user simply types the razón social by hand.
#
# Which provider to ask:
#   apisnet   (default) https://api.apis.net.pe — works with no token at all,
#             but that anonymous endpoint is rate-limited per source IP (429
#             after a handful of calls). A free token raises the quota and
#             switches to the documented /v2/sunat/ruc endpoint.
#   decolecta https://api.decolecta.com — requires a paid token.
#   auto      decolecta when DECOLECTA_API_TOKEN exists, otherwise apisnet.
RUC_API_PROVIDER=apisnet

# Only fill the one that matches the provider above.
APISNET_API_TOKEN=
DECOLECTA_API_TOKEN=

# Upstream call timeout, in ms. Default 8000.
# RUC_API_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# How long a successful lookup is cached in memory, in ms. Default 24 h.
# Razón social changes rarely, and the cache is what keeps the server inside
# the provider's rate limit during a registration spike.
# RUC_API_CACHE_TTL_MS=86400000

# --- Server -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Relaxes the login limiter (5 -> 500 attempts per 15 min) for local work.
# `npm run dev` sets it, so `npm test:e2e` and a manual dev session can log in
# repeatedly. NEVER set it in production, and never derive it from NODE_ENV:
# `NODE_ENV !== "production"` is also true for an unset or misspelled value, which
# would raise the login ceiling 100x on a live deployment without anyone noticing.
# RATE_LIMIT_RELAXED=true

# --- Anti-spam (self-hosted CAPTCHA) ----------------------------------------
#
# The public endpoints (complaint intake, public AI assistant, ARCO intake,
# registration) are gated by a self-hosted proof-of-work challenge: the server
# sends a salt + difficulty, the browser finds the nonce whose
# SHA-256(salt+nonce) starts with that many hex zeros, and the server re-hashes
# it. Nothing to read or type for the consumer, no third-party script, and
# nothing that a bot can parse out of the response - see
# server/services/captchaService.ts.
#
# Leading hex zeros required (1-6). Each extra zero multiplies the work by 16:
#   4 = ~65k hashes, a few hundred ms in a browser (default)
#   5 = ~1M hashes, noticeable on a low-end phone - use only under attack
#   6 = ~16M hashes, only if the spam is relentless
# Raising it slows bots AND real consumers, so raise it temporarily and lower it
# again once the wave passes.
# CAPTCHA_POW_DIFFICULTY=4

# Upper bound of the nonce search space. Default 2000000.
# CAPTCHA_POW_MAX_NUMBER=2000000

# Minimum time, in ms, between issuing a challenge and accepting its solution.
# OFF by default (0), which is deliberate: raising it rejects anything that fills
# the form quickly - autofill, a password manager, a fast keyboard-only visitor -
# and on a legally mandated complaint form a false rejection costs more than the
# spam it stops (the puzzle, the honeypot and the rate limit already price that
# spam). Set it only to answer a specific wave of bots that submit within
# milliseconds of receiving a challenge. The AI assistant and registration always
# opt out, since they solve on demand.
# CAPTCHA_MIN_DWELL_MS=0

PORT=5000
